      "spam": false,
      "statusChangeID": null,
      "value": "For anyone reading this, there is an obscure solution to this issue that has been known:\n\nSomeone just pointed out how they're having difficulties continuously teleporting themselves without spinning themselves out of control.\n\nAs answer has been known for a while, which is to use TeleportTo(..., ..., SpawnOrientation.AlignRoomWithSpawnPoint) and GetTrackingData(VRCPlayerApi.TrackingDataType.Origin), however, I can't find any pre-existing written article or documentation online that clearly explains how you're supposed to do this.\n\nSo here's an attempt to write that down:\n\nThe function \"TeleportTo\" has a variant that accepts a SpawnOrientation parameter. One of these parameters is AlignRoomWithSpawnPoint. To simplify, this roughly means that it teleports the player's room instead of teleporting the player.\n\nThe solution, as far as I know, is to teleport the center of your room playspace, so that your position inside your room will match the destination.\n\nTherefore you need to calculate the position of the center of your room playspace at the destination.\n\nPlease correct it in the comments you believe it's misleading or has inaccuracies.\n\nhttps://gist.github.com/hai-vr/8bfa8f2cbb73d211e790dc75045c96a4\n\n```csharp\n\nprivate static void StableTeleportTo(Vector3 destinationPos)\n\n{\n\n    var playerPos = Networking.LocalPlayer.GetPosition();\n\n    var playerOrigin = Networking.LocalPlayer.GetTrackingData(VRCPlayerApi.TrackingDataType.Origin);\n\n    var posToOrigin = playerOrigin.position - playerPos;\n\n    var destinationOrigin = destinationPos + posToOrigin;\n\n    Networking.LocalPlayer.TeleportTo(\n\n        destinationOrigin,\n\n        playerOrigin.rotation,\n\n        VRC_SceneDescriptor.SpawnOrientation.AlignRoomWithSpawnPoint\n\n    );\n\n}\n\n```",
      "viewerReactions": null,
      "voteID": null
    },
    {
      "__v": 0,
      "_id": "6507b2e268fe7e0991c3ad17",
      "author": {
        "_id": "6106daad00a2c06a55948f85",
        "alias": "Nectar Thrush",
        "aliasID": "65cc0dcded6eefce4c9f8d64",
        "anonymized": false,
        "avatarURL": "https://canny-assets.io/images/ef5583621ed5d0757e48c72158e06cb3.png",
        "created": "2021-08-01T17:32:29.144Z",
        "name": "Sylan Troh",
        "serviceAccount": false,
        "urlName": "<redacted>"
      },
      "authorID": null,
      "boardID": "5df404cc2072644ac4b5e385",
      "companyID": "58c62e995865713647a7115d",
      "created": "2023-09-18T02:16:02.297Z",
      "deleted": false,
      "fileURLs": [],
      "files": [],
      "imageURLs": [],
      "integrationSourceType": null,
      "internal": false,
      "itemSourceType": null,
      "mentionedUserIDs": null,
      "mentionedUsers": [],
      "mergeID": null,
      "parentID": null,
      "pinned": false,
      "postDeleted": false,
      "postID": "5e657c9fa6417569289ffb07",
      "private": false,
      "reactions": null,
      "sourceLink": null,
      "spam": false,
      "statusChangeID": null,
      "value": "Using AlignRoomWithSpawnPoint seems to introduce this problem: https://feedback.vrchat.com/vrchat-udon-closed-alpha-bugs/p/teleport-sometimes-gets-stuck-on-geometry. This was not usable for my use case, as I am working on a \"noclip\" system, so getting stuck on geometry entirely defeats the purpose.\n\nInstead I'm using the following function which teleports the player, calculates the difference between the expected rotation, and where they actually ended up, then teleports them again.\n\n```\n\npublic void TeleportPlayer(VRCPlayerApi player, Vector3 teleportPos, Quaternion teleportRotation, bool lerpOnRemote)\n\n{\n\n    player.TeleportTo(\n\n        teleportPos,\n\n        teleportRotation,\n\n        VRC_SceneDescriptor.SpawnOrientation.AlignPlayerWithSpawnPoint,\n\n        lerpOnRemote\n\n        );\n\n    player.TeleportTo(teleportPos,\n\n        Quaternion.Inverse(localPlayer.GetRotation()) * teleportRotation * teleportRotation, \n\n        VRC_SceneDescriptor.SpawnOrientation.AlignPlayerWithSpawnPoint,\n\n        lerpOnRemote);\n\n}\n\n```\n\nThis avoids getting stuck on geometry, and avoids rotation in most cases. There are still some angles that I can turn my head at which I can get some rotation to occur, but instead of uncontrollable spinning, it's only a small amount. I must admit, teleporting twice is a ridiculous \"fix\" but it is the only thing I've found that works.",
      "viewerReactions": null,
      "voteID": null
    }
  ],
  "companyID": "58c62e995865713647a7115d",
